<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Enrico Fermi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1901–1954</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Enrico Fermi was an Italian physicist who built the first nuclear reactor. Working in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, he showed that slow neutrons could induce radioactivity and received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physics in 1938. Because his wife was Jewish and the Fascist racial laws threatened his family, he moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> after the prize ceremony, where he led the team that achieved the first controlled nuclear chain reaction in Chicago in 1942.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/enrico-fermi.jpg" alt="Enrico Fermi" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Department of Energy-Office of Public Affairs, restored by Yann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enrico_Fermi_1943-49.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
